From a60a1fc5e79d25d45c2d8f2831b757a3f2b1687f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Alessandro Fontana <alessandro.fontana@pm.me>
Date: Fri, 28 Aug 2026 18:40:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Input: stmfts - do not wait for a ready event on resume

stmfts_wait_for_ready() belongs to the reset path.  After a system reset
the controller announces itself with STMFTS_EV_CONTROLLER_READY and the
poll returns in milliseconds.  After STMFTS_SLEEP_OUT it announces
nothing at all, so the poll drains an empty FIFO for the full
STMFTS_READY_TIMEOUT_MS and then reports a failure that is not one:

  stmfts 2-0049: timeout waiting for ready, last event 00
  stmfts 2-0049: no ready event after resume: -110

That the controller is awake regardless is visible in the interrupt
enable register, which still reads back the value written before
suspend: sleep-in never powered it down, so there is no boot to
announce.  Accepting STMFTS_EV_SLEEP_OUT_CONTROLLER_READY as well does
not help, because no event is emitted on this path.

The cost is not just two seconds of log noise.  For the whole duration
of the poll the attention interrupt is still disabled and multi-touch
sensing is still off, since both are re-enabled after the wait returns.
A user who touches the panel as soon as the screen comes back gets
nothing, and only the second touch registers.

Replace the poll with a short fixed delay.  The reset path, where the
announcement is real and the timeout is a genuine error, is untouched.
---
 drivers/input/touchscreen/stmfts.c | 10 +++++++---
 1 file changed, 7 insertions(+), 3 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/input/touchscreen/stmfts.c b/drivers/input/touchscreen/stmfts.c
index 49bc03bc02..a749b17ee8 100644
--- a/drivers/input/touchscreen/stmfts.c
+++ b/drivers/input/touchscreen/stmfts.c
@@ -1097,9 +1097,13 @@ static int stmfts_resume(struct device *dev)
 		return err;
 	}
 
-	err = stmfts_wait_for_ready(sdata);
-	if (err)
-		dev_warn(dev, "no ready event after resume: %d\n", err);
+	/*
+	 * Sleep-out is not acknowledged with a ready event on this
+	 * generation: the controller was never powered down, so it has no
+	 * boot to announce.  Polling for one only delays re-enabling the
+	 * interrupt and sensing below, which costs the first touch.
+	 */
+	stmfts_delay(20);
 
 	stmfts_command(sdata, STMFTS_CLEAR_EVENT_STACK);
 	stmfts_command(sdata, STMFTS_MS_MT_SENSE_ON);
-- 
